Gestational Diabetes
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Asymptomatic presentation, Fetal macrosomia, Elevated oral glucose tolerance test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-017
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of profound symptomatic hyperglycemia (blurred vision, extreme thirst) and acute panic at 28 weeks gestation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a third-trimester pregnant patient experiencing sudden blurred vision, intractable thirst, and a home glucometer reading of 16.5 mmol/L, causing a severe panic attack regarding fetal safety. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to immediately rule out diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), assess fetal wellbeing, and initiate glycemic triage to prevent maternal/fetal compromise before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My vision suddenly went blurry, my mouth is completely dry no matter how much I drink, and my partner's blood sugar machine said 16.5.
History of Present Illness: The patient, currently at 28 weeks gestation, had a previously completely asymptomatic presentation regarding her blood sugar until 48 hours ago. She reports a sudden worsening of polydipsia with excessive thirst and polyuria well beyond typical pregnancy-related frequency. Tonight, she developed blurred vision and lightheadedness, prompting the use of her partner's home glucometer. She reports maternal weight gain exceeding expected guidelines and a recent ultrasound noting early fetal macrosomia and mild polyhydramnios. She also complains of severe, recurrent candidal vaginitis due to suspected glycosuria, causing intense sleep-depriving pruritus tonight.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, but highly agitated and pacing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute health anxiety regarding fetal demise.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/78 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Patient appears flushed, anxious, and is constantly drinking water from a large jug.
Physical Exam: Mucous membranes are slightly dry. Abdomen is gravid, soft, and non-tender. Fetal heart tones are auscultated via handheld Doppler at 145 bpm, with a regular rhythm and good variability. Point-of-care capillary glucose is 15.8 mmol/L. Point-of-care urinalysis demonstrates gross glycosuria (4+) but is entirely negative for ketones and protein.
Environmental Observations: Partner is supportive; multiple empty water bottles present in the room.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking prenatal vitamins. Applied over-the-counter clotrimazole cream for vaginitis with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Undiagnosed Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (Acute symptomatic hyperglycemia without ketoacidosis) - Status: Acute onset
Differentials:

Diabetic Ketoacidosis - Definitively ruled out by the complete absence of ketonuria on point-of-care testing and a normal, unlabored respiratory rate (no Kussmaul breathing).

Pre-eclampsia - Ruled out due to normotensive blood pressure readings, absence of proteinuria, and lack of right upper quadrant pain.

Urinary Tract Infection - Ruled out by negative nitrites and leukocyte esterase on urinalysis; polyuria is purely osmotic.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk requires urgent morning follow-up)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with profound, acute symptomatic hyperglycemia (osmotic diuresis, visual changes) at 28 weeks gestation, confirmed by a point-of-care glucose of 15.8 mmol/L. While classical gestational diabetes is often an asymptomatic presentation identified via an elevated oral glucose tolerance test, this acute symptomatic manifestation required immediate after-hours intervention. The absolute necessity to rule out life-threatening diabetic ketoacidosis via urinalysis, confirm fetal viability via Doppler, and de-escalate severe maternal panic satisfied the criteria for urgent assessment. Leaving this patient unassessed until the in-hours period would have risked progression to DKA and caused unbearable psychological distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassured the patient of normal fetal heart tones. Hydrated with 1L of oral plain water while observing. Prescribed a 150mg fluconazole oral tablet (single dose, permissible in 2nd/3rd trimester for severe, refractory cases) to immediately treat the severe candidal vaginitis. Generated a priority, fast-tracked referral to the hospital's high-risk obstetric/endocrine clinic for tomorrow morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: To assist in stabilizing your blood sugar tonight, completely eliminate all fruit juices, sodas, and simple carbohydrates immediately; observing the absence of further blurred vision will reinforce strict dietary compliance. To monitor fetal wellbeing, perform formal fetal kick counts twice daily (lying quietly for an hour); feeling the baby move consistently will directly reduce your acute anxiety.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Urgent contact with the local hospital's maternity assessment unit or high-risk obstetric clinic required at 0800 hours tomorrow.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if you develop continuous vomiting, rapid, deep breathing, severe abdominal pain, or if you notice a sudden, significant decrease in fetal movements.

